{
  "info": {
    "name": "CIDCO Architect Handshake & Token Flow",
    "description": "End-to-end handshake, token and data flow between an architect and the CIDCO admin.\n\n## CASE 1 \u2014 first-time setup\n1. **Admin > Officer login**\n2. **Admin > Issue handshake credentials** \u2014 saves clientId + clientSecret\n3. **Architect > Validate \u2014 success** \u2014 send credentials + your station IP/device. CIDCO whitelists them and returns BOTH tokens (saved to {{accessToken}} / {{refreshToken}}).\n4. **Architect > Send AQI data** \u2014 Bearer {{accessToken}}. Schedule this every 3 hours.\n\n## CASE 2 \u2014 access token expires\n'Send AQI data' answers **503 / ACCESS_EXPIRED**. Run **Architect > Refresh access token** \u2014 you get a new access token and keep the same refresh token. Resume sending.\n\n## CASE 3 \u2014 refresh token expires\nOnce the refresh token lapses the access token dies with it, so 'Send AQI data' answers **503 / BOTH_EXPIRED**. Re-run **Architect > Validate \u2014 success** with clientId + clientSecret to get a brand-new pair, then carry on from CASE 1 step 4.\n\n## Admin controls\n**Set token policy** changes the access/refresh validity windows; **Generate token** issues a full pair from the dashboard. Both take effect on the API immediately.\n\nAdmin requests use the officer session cookie; architect requests use only credentials/tokens, exactly as an external system would.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "variable": [
    {
      "key": "baseUrl",
      "value": "http://localhost:3000"
    },
    {
      "key": "adminEmail",
      "value": "officer@cidco.example"
    },
    {
      "key": "adminPassword",
      "value": "Password123"
    },
    {
